%I A397936 #25 Aug 05 2026 22:33:35
%S A397936 0,5,36,257,1800,12605,88236,617657,4323600,30265205,211856436,
%T A397936 1482995057,10380965400,72666757805,508667304636,3560671132457,
%U A397936 24924697927200,174472885490405,1221310198432836,8549171389029857,59844199723209000,418909398062463005
%N A397936 a(n) = floor(3*7^n/4).
%C A397936 For n >= 1, the base-7 representation of a(n) consists of the first n digits of the base-7 expansion of 3/4 = 0.515151..._7, i.e., n alternating digits 5,1,5,1,... beginning with 5.
%C A397936 The base-7 digits of a(n) and A397937(n) sum to 6 in every position.
%C A397936 Base-7 analog of A081251 (base 3).
%C A397936 For odd b the base-b expansion of 1/4 has minimal period the multiplicative order of b mod 4: a single repeated digit when b == 1 (mod 4), a 2-digit block when b == 3 (mod 4). Corresponding digits of 1/4 and 3/4 sum to b-1 in both cases, so it is the alternation, not the complementarity, that distinguishes b == 3 (mod 4).
%C A397936 Both smaller odd bases degenerate: for b == 1 (mod 4), floor(3*b^n/4) = 3*floor(b^n/4) identically (b = 5: A125833(n) = 3*A003463(n)); for b = 3, floor(3*3^n/4) = floor(3^(n+1)/4), so the pair coincides up to offset (A081251); no other base can, since floor(3*b^n/4)/floor(b^(n+k)/4) -> 3/b^k, equal to 1 only for b = 3, k = 1. Base 7 is the smallest odd base for which floor(b^n/4) and floor(3*b^n/4) are neither proportional nor index shifts.
%H A397936 Reed L. Rucka, <a href="/A397936/b397936.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%H A397936 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (7,1,-7).
%F A397936 a(n) = (3*7^n - (-1)^n - 2)/4.
%F A397936 a(n) = 7*a(n-1) + 3 - 2*(-1)^n for n >= 1, a(0) = 0.
%F A397936 a(n) = 7*a(n-1) + a(n-2) - 7*a(n-3) for n >= 3.
%F A397936 a(n) + A397937(n) = A024075(n).
%F A397936 a(n) + a(n+1) = A198688(n).
%F A397936 a(n) = A083232(n) - (1 + (-1)^n)/2.
%F A397936 binomial(7^n - 1, a(n)) == (-1)^n (mod 7) for n >= 1 (Lucas: 7^n - 1 is all 6's in base 7 and the base-7 digits of a(n) are all 5 or 1, with binomial(6,5) = binomial(6,1) = 6 == -1 (mod 7), so every position contributes a factor -1).
%F A397936 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/7^n = 3/4.
%F A397936 G.f.: x*(5 + x)/((1 - x)*(1 + x)*(1 - 7*x)).
%F A397936 E.g.f.: (3*exp(7*x) - 2*exp(x) - exp(-x))/4. - _Stefano Spezia_, Jul 17 2026
%e A397936 a(3) = 257 = 515 in base 7; a(4) = 1800 = 5151 in base 7.
%p A397936 seq(floor(3*7^n/4), n=0..20);
%t A397936 Table[Floor[3*7^n/4], {n, 0, 20}]
%o A397936 (PARI) a(n) = 3*7^n\4;
%Y A397936 Cf. A081251 (base-3 analog), A083232 (nearest integer to 3*7^n/4 instead of floor), A198688 (consecutive-term sums), A000420, A397937 (complement within 7^n - 1).
%K A397936 nonn,easy,base,new
%O A397936 0,2
%A A397936 _Reed L. Rucka_, Jul 15 2026
